    ]}
  />

  <h2>Atumelnant at a glance</h2>
  <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
      <tbody class="align-top">
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="w-1/3 py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">What it is</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">A once-daily oral, non-peptide antagonist of the ACTH receptor (MC2R)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Also called</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">CRN04894</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Developer</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">Crinetics Pharmaceuticals</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Lead indication</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">Classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia — Phase 3 (CALM-CAH) enrolling; a paediatric study (BALANCE-CAH) has started</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--border)]">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Second indication</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">ACTH-dependent Cushing’s syndrome — earlier-stage, still enrolling</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Status</td>
          <td class="py-2 text-[var(--text)]">Investigational. Not approved.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Most melanocortin drugs are agonists. They copy <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> or{' '}
    <a href="/acth">ACTH</a> and turn a receptor on. <strong>Atumelnant</strong> is the
    exception that has gone furthest in the clinic: an oral small molecule built to
    <em>block</em> <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>, the one receptor that answers to
    ACTH and almost nothing else.<Cite n={5} /> If the rest of this site is about switching
    the system on, this page is about switching one lock off.
  </p>

  <h2>Why block the adrenal’s receptor</h2>
  <p>
    In <a href="/congenital-adrenal-hyperplasia">classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia</a>{' '}
    a missing enzyme, usually 21-hydroxylase, means the adrenal cannot finish making
    cortisol. The pituitary, hearing no cortisol, keeps releasing ACTH. That ACTH lands on
    MC2R and drives the gland to grow and to spill its backed-up precursors into
    androgens.<Cite n={1} /> The usual treatment replaces cortisol and uses extra
    glucocorticoid to hold ACTH down — often more steroid than the body would have made on
    its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are two ways to cut that loop without simply adding more steroid. One, already
    approved, is upstream: crinecerfont blocks the pituitary receptor that releases ACTH,
    so less of the hormone is made.<Cite n={6} /> The other is at the gland itself. Block
    MC2R and the ACTH that is already circulating cannot turn steroidogenesis on. That is
    atumelnant’s bet, and it is why the same molecule is also being studied in
    ACTH-dependent <a href="/cushings-disease">Cushing’s</a>, where the problem is too much
    ACTH from a tumour rather than a missing enzyme.<Cite n={5} />
  </p>
  <p>
    MC2R is an unusually clean target for this. It needs both ACTH and the accessory
    protein MRAP, and the other four melanocortin receptors do not read ACTH as their
    ligand. An antagonist built for that address is, in principle, an adrenal drug rather
    than a whole-family one. The constraint is the obvious one: a person still needs
    cortisol. The antagonist is meant to be paired with replacement, not to abolish the
    adrenal.
  </p>

  <h2>What Phase 2 actually showed</h2>
  <p>
    The adult CAH study (NCT05907291) was open-label and twelve weeks long. The first
    published interim report described rapid, sustained falls in androstenedione and
    17-hydroxyprogesterone.<Cite n={1} /> The sponsor’s full readout at ENDO 2026 split the
    result into two designs, and it should be read as a company presentation, not as a
    peer-reviewed controlled trial.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the cohorts that kept each person’s glucocorticoid dose unchanged, mean morning
    androstenedione fell about 58% at 40 mg once daily (11 people), about 70% at 80 mg
    (11), and about 80% at 120 mg (6).<Cite n={2} /> A later cohort took 80 mg and, from
    week 2, had the steroid dose stepped down toward a physiologic target. At week 12 the
    mean androstenedione change was about −67%, and seven of the eight people who finished
    were on a physiologic glucocorticoid dose.<Cite n={2} /> The sponsor also reported no
    treatment-related severe or serious adverse events in the programme to that point.
    Numbers this small, without a placebo arm, can move. They are a reason to run Phase 3,
    not a substitute for it.
  </p>

  <h2>The trials that have to decide it</h2>
  <p>
    CALM-CAH (NCT07144163) is a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in
    adults with classic CAH. Participants are assigned 2:1 to atumelnant 80 mg or placebo
    once daily for 32 weeks, with an option to go to 120 mg, while glucocorticoid doses
    are tapered toward a physiologic range. The primary endpoint is the proportion who, at
    week 32, have morning androstenedione at or below the upper limit of normal{' '}
    <em>and</em> are on physiologic glucocorticoid replacement.<Cite n={3} /> The first
    patient was dosed in December 2025.<Cite n={4} /> A paediatric study, BALANCE-CAH, has
    also been started.<Cite n={5} /> Neither has reported results.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cushing’s is thinner. Early open-label work, run with the NIH and described on the{' '}
    <a href="/cushings-disease">Cushing’s page</a>, saw cortisol fall within days and then
    required replacement — a pharmacology result in a handful of people, not a treatment
    trial. The sponsor says a later study in ACTH-dependent Cushing’s is enrolling.<Cite
      n={5}
    /> What a pituitary tumour does when the adrenal stops answering is one of the
    questions that study has to face.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it sits</h2>
  <p>
    Atumelnant is the adrenal entry on the{' '}
    <a href="/melanocortin-antagonists">antagonists hub</a>, next to the MC4R blockers
    aimed at <a href="/cachexia">cachexia</a>. The biology it depends on is on{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a> and <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>. Status across the
    family is on the <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-8 rounded-lg border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--accent-soft)] p-6 text-sm leading-relaxed">
    <p class="font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Education, not a protocol</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--text)]">
      This page explains an investigational drug. It is not medical advice and gives no
      dosing or access instructions. Atumelnant is not approved. Congenital adrenal
      hyperplasia and Cushing’s require specialist care, and an adrenal crisis is a medical
      emergency. See the <a href="/about">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

